HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (italic = abstract, dashed border = interface, '
 '&laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. '
 '<b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = extends or implements. Filled diamond = owns: the bank owns every account, '
 'an account owns every row of its ledger. Plain arrow = references: an account points at its status and its kind, a '
 'row at its type. Dashed lavender = handed in through <code>configure()</code>, <code>addRule()</code> or '
 '<code>setFeePolicy()</code>. Dotted blue = notifies. <b>Where state lives:</b> the account has the balance, the '
 'status, the ledger, its rules and the one lock, and its private <code>post()</code> is the only thing in the system '
 'that writes a row; the bank has the accounts, the transfer receipts, the interest rules, the clock and no balance '
 'of its own; a row never changes after it is written; <code>Money</code> is immutable, so a number in a ledger row '
 'cannot be edited behind the account\'s back. Notice what is <i>not</i> here: no Balance class, because a balance is '
 'a field the account writes with the row that explains it; no Transfer class, because a transfer is a method on the '
 'bank plus a receipt; and almost nothing in SavingsAccount and CurrentAccount, because the sentence they disagree '
 'about became a rule object instead of an overridden method.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the mechanics. Each copy '
 'button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s reference code, with '
 'an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (thirty-three claims proven; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (how money is represented, and what actually differs '
 'between savings and current, come first); then type in the order of Main.java: the three enums, the exception family, '
 'the Money value type, the Txn and Statement records, the Clock, the WithdrawalRule interface with MinBalanceRule and '
 'OverdraftRule, the InterestRule with SimpleInterest and the PromoInterest wrapper, the listener interface, then '
 'Account with its one lock and its single <code>post()</code>, then the two thin subclasses, then Bank with the '
 'ordered locks and the seven-step transfer, then a main with the race.</div></div>')

FU = [
("The bank announces a festival bonus: half a per cent on top of the savings rate, for one month only.", "twist", 5,
 "Nothing is edited. PromoInterest takes an InterestRule and returns that rule's interest plus a bonus computed the "
 "same way, so the base rate and the promotion stay separate objects and the promotion comes off again by dropping "
 "one wrapper. The bank is handed <code>new PromoInterest(new SimpleInterest(400), 50)</code> for SAVINGS and nothing "
 "else in the system changes: not Account, not Bank, not the tests. This is the Decorator, and it is worth pointing "
 "at the alternative &mdash; an <code>if (festivalMonth)</code> inside SimpleInterest &mdash; which is the same "
 "feature and a permanent edit to a class that had one job.",
 sect(src, "final class PromoInterest", "interface FeePolicy")),
("Two people transfer between the same two accounts at the same instant, in opposite directions. Prove you cannot lose a rupee, or deadlock.", "non-functional", 10,
 "Two separate guarantees, one test. No lost update: the rule checks, the row and the balance all happen inside one "
 "hold of the account's lock, so no other writer can run in the gap between reading a balance and writing it. No "
 "deadlock: a transfer takes both locks in account-id order, lower id first, which makes the wait graph acyclic &mdash; "
 "A-then-B and B-then-A become the same order, so a cycle cannot form. The proof is arithmetic rather than a count: "
 "sixty-four threads wait on one latch, fire transfers in both directions between four accounts, and the test then "
 "adds up every balance in the bank. A lost update would change the total, because a lost credit has no matching lost "
 "debit. The deadlock proof is the timeout on <code>Future.get</code>: a cycle would make the test hang rather than "
 "fail, so the timeout is the assertion.",
 T("        // 4. sixty-four threads firing transfers in BOTH directions", "        // 5. an account nobody touches")),
("One lock per account, and two locks for a transfer. Does that scale, or have you serialised the bank?", "non-functional", 5,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic. The locked part of a posting is about twelve operations &mdash; a status "
 "check, one to three rule checks, a subtraction, an append and three field writes &mdash; roughly a microsecond and "
 "a half; a transfer is that twice under two locks, about three microseconds. A salary account sees thirty postings a "
 "month. Even an invented thousand postings a second into one account holds its lock one and a half milliseconds in "
 "every second: fifteen hundredths of one per cent. And the lock is per account, so the number of accounts is "
 "irrelevant; only the two accounts in a transfer ever share. The ladder, in order: keep the SMS, the audit file and "
 "any database write outside the lock, which this code already does; if one account really is hot, split its balance "
 "into k sub-balances each with its own lock and sum on read; and beyond one process, make the debit one conditional "
 "UPDATE with the rule in the WHERE clause.",
 sect(src, "    TransferReceipt transfer(String transferId", "    TransferReceipt receipt(String transferId)")),
("The destination account is frozen, and you only find out halfway. What is the state of both accounts?", "functional", 10,
 "Exactly what it was, because you never find out halfway: the destination is asked everything that could refuse a "
 "credit &mdash; frozen, closed, wrong currency &mdash; before a single paise leaves the source. The order is the "
 "design, not a detail. If that check throws, no row has been written anywhere, the transfer id is <i>not</i> claimed, "
 "and the caller can retry the same id once the account is unfrozen. The same shape covers a rule saying no: the "
 "source's rules run inside its own hold, before its row is written, so a refusal leaves no row, no paise and no "
 "alert. The one case left is a credit that somehow throws after the debit, which cannot happen here; the code still "
 "posts a REVERSAL row on the source rather than leaving money in mid-air, because an audit trail you can edit is not "
 "one &mdash; you undo by writing the opposite row.",
 T("        // a frozen destination is discovered BEFORE the source is debited", "        // rules compose")),
("The app asks for a balance a thousand times a second, and then wants March's statement. Make both fast.", "non-functional", 5,
 "The balance is a stored field, written in the same lock hold as the row that changed it, so a balance read is one "
 "field read: O(1), and it can never disagree with the ledger because the two move together. The statement is the "
 "interesting one: rows are appended in time order and never reordered, so both ends of a window are a binary search "
 "and the answer is a sublist &mdash; O(log n + k) for k rows, never a scan of a lifetime of history. The opening and "
 "closing balances come free, because every row carries the balance after it. Reads take the account's lock so a "
 "number is never read half-written, which costs tens of nanoseconds; the day that matters you keep the balance in an "
 "AtomicLong and drop the lock on reads.",
 sect(src, "    final Statement statement(long fromMs", "    /** A copy of the whole ledger")),
("Mid-round: \"cap ATM withdrawals at INR 3,000 a day.\" And while you are there, KYC.", "functional", 8,
 "One new class and one line, for both. A daily cap is stateful &mdash; it has to remember what today already cost "
 "&mdash; and that memory has nowhere clean to live if the veto is an overridden method; as a rule object it is a "
 "private counter updated through the <code>onWithdrawn</code> hook, which only fires after the row is written, so a "
 "refused withdrawal never counts against the cap. It composes with the minimum balance, because every rule on the "
 "account must agree. KYC is the same move with a different sentence: a status on the rule, a cap while the papers "
 "are not in order, and nothing in Account or Bank changes. Both are called inside the account's lock, which is also "
 "why their fields need no locking of their own.",
 X("a daily cap on cash", "a joint account") + "\n" + X("KYC --", "a fee on a transfer")),
("A joint account: two owners, and anything over INR 50,000 needs the second signature.", "twist", 10,
 "The only genuinely new state is the set of owners; everything that makes it a joint account is a rule. Below the "
 "threshold a withdrawal simply goes through. Above it, the rule refuses until a second, <i>different</i> owner has "
 "signed for that exact amount: the first owner's call parks the request and returns null, and the second owner's "
 "call performs the withdrawal. The signature is spent through the same <code>onWithdrawn</code> hook the daily cap "
 "uses, so one approval can never pay for two withdrawals. The balance, the ledger, the lock and the posting path are "
 "the ones every other account uses &mdash; which is the point of the whole page: a twist that needs a big block of "
 "code means the derivation went wrong.",
 X("a joint account", "a standing instruction")),
("Rent on the first of every month. And then: model a loan.", "twist", 8,
 "Both are schedules that post ordinary transfers, and the interesting part of both is the transfer id. A standing "
 "instruction builds its id out of its own name and the period number, so running the job twice &mdash; or running it "
 "on two servers at the same moment &mdash; pays each period exactly once, because a transfer is idempotent by that "
 "id. A loan is not a new kind of money either: it is an account whose balance is negative and a schedule of "
 "instalments, each one a transfer from the borrower's account to the loan account, with the same id trick. This "
 "version uses equal principal plus interest on the reducing outstanding, so every number stays exact integer paise "
 "and the last instalment absorbs the rounding, closing the loan at exactly zero.",
 X("a standing instruction", "a loan as a schedule") + "\n" + X("a loan as a schedule", "KYC")),
("Charge INR 25 on any transfer over a lakh. The fee must never be separated from the transfer.", "twist", 5,
 "The fee policy is another one-method rule handed in, and the design point is not the policy, it is where the row is "
 "written: <code>Bank.transfer</code> asks the policy inside the same two-lock section that moved the money, so the "
 "transfer and its charge commit together or not at all, and the FEE row carries the transfer id as its reference. "
 "Doing it after the unlock would be a second, separate truth that can be lost. Note also that the fee row is posted "
 "without publishing, and the bank publishes it after releasing both locks, like everything else.",
 X("a fee on a transfer", "multi-currency")),
("Two people paid in dollars. Support other currencies.", "twist", 5,
 "Money already carries its currency and throws rather than adding INR to USD, so nothing can drift quietly; an "
 "account holds exactly one currency and refuses a posting in another. What is missing is one injected rule: what a "
 "rupee buys right now. Rates are quoted in basis points so the arithmetic stays in longs, and the conversion happens "
 "at exactly one moment &mdash; between the debit and the credit, inside the two-lock section &mdash; so yesterday's "
 "balances are never re-priced when the rate moves. The rate and the source amount go on the row, which is how a "
 "statement is still explainable a year later. Account, the lock and the rules do not change: this is move 5 (a new "
 "key on the collection) plus an injected rule.",
 X("multi-currency", "persistence and a second server")),
("Persist it. And now there are two branches, on two servers.", "twist", 8,
 "The balance becomes a row behind a repository interface and the account's code does not change, only what it was "
 "handed. The debit, which is a read-modify-write under a lock today, becomes one conditional UPDATE whose WHERE "
 "clause carries the rule: <code>SET paise = paise - ? WHERE id = ? AND paise - ? &gt;= floor</code>. That is the "
 "database performing the same compare-and-set the lock was doing, so two servers cannot both succeed and no "
 "application lock is needed. The transfer id gets a unique index, so a retried request inserts nothing the second "
 "time &mdash; the same idempotency the receipt map gives us in memory, moved one level down. Both rows move in one "
 "transaction, which is the database's version of holding both locks.",
 X("persistence and a second server", "Runs every extension")),
("Where does time come from, and how do you test interest and dormancy without waiting six months?", "design", 3,
 "The bank is handed a Clock and hands it to every account it opens; nothing in the system reads the wall clock "
 "inside a method. A test therefore owns the date: it keeps a one-element array, hands in a lambda that reads it, "
 "moves it forward by a year and asserts that INR 100,000 at four per cent earned exactly INR 4,000.00 &mdash; in "
 "integer paise, so the assertion is an equality and not a tolerance. The same seam drives dormancy (move the clock "
 "two hundred days, run the sweep, and the account is DORMANT), the daily cap (move it one day and the allowance is "
 "back) and the statement window. It is also why <code>configure</code> refuses to run after the first account "
 "exists: the clock must be the same one everywhere.",
 "/** Where time comes from. Injected everywhere, so a test can say \"six months later\" without sleeping. */\n"
 "interface Clock { long nowMs(); }\n\n"
 "// on the bank: handed in with the rules, then given to every account it opens\n"
 "void configure(Map<AccountKind, InterestRule> rules, Clock clock) {\n"
 "    if (!accounts.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException(\"configure before opening accounts\");\n"
 "    interestRules.clear(); interestRules.putAll(rules); this.clock = clock;\n"
 "}\n\n"
 "// in a test: pick the instant, then move it\n"
 "long[] now = { 1_700_000_000_000L };\n"
 "bank.configure(Map.of(AccountKind.SAVINGS, new SimpleInterest(400)), () -> now[0]);\n"
 "SavingsAccount a = bank.openSavings(\"SB-3\", \"c1\", Money.inr(\"100000.00\"), Money.inr(\"0.00\"));\n"
 "now[0] += 365L * 24 * 3600 * 1000;                      // a year later\n"
 "bank.accrueInterest(365);                                // -> exactly INR 4000.00 of interest\n"
 "now[0] += 200L * 24 * 3600 * 1000;                      // and two hundred days of silence\n"
 "bank.markDormant();                                      // -> DORMANT, until the next customer posting\n"),
("Which pattern is where, which SOLID letter is where, and why are Savings and Current subclasses at all?", "design", 8,
 "None of the patterns was chosen up front; each is what a move produced. Strategy is move 3: the withdrawal veto, the "
 "interest rule and the fee policy are one-method interfaces handed in. Decorator is the same move's wrapper, "
 "PromoInterest. Observer is move 4's rule that an SMS must never be inside the lock. State is move 6, the account's "
 "life. The ordered two-lock acquisition has no pattern name and is the most valuable line in the file. For SOLID: S "
 "is move 2 (Money does arithmetic, Txn is one row, Account guards one balance, Bank owns the two-account invariant); "
 "O is the daily cap, a new file and one addRule call; L is that deposit and withdraw are final, so no subclass can "
 "skip a rule; I is five one-method interfaces; D is configure() and addRule(), which is why a test can hand in a "
 "clock from next March. As for the subclasses: they survive because interest and an overdraft ceiling are genuinely "
 "is-a differences, but notice how little is left in them &mdash; each is a constructor that wires one rule. The "
 "veto itself is deliberately not an overridden method, because an override expresses one difference and cannot "
 "compose with the second and third. Factory earns its place the day account kinds arrive as config strings; Builder "
 "earns its place the day a real application form arrives with a dozen optional fields.",
 sect(src, "final class SavingsAccount", "/**\n * The bank: it owns the customers") + "\n"
 "// Strategy: the veto is an object, so vetoes COMPOSE -- which an overridden method cannot do\n"
 "asha.addRule(new MinBalanceRule(Money.inr(\"1000.00\")));\n"
 "asha.addRule(new DailyLimitRule(Money.inr(\"3000.00\")));   // both must agree, at run time\n\n"
 "// Decorator: a bonus on top of a rate nobody edited\n"
 "bank.configure(Map.of(AccountKind.SAVINGS, new PromoInterest(new SimpleInterest(400), 50)), clock);\n\n"
 "// Observer: the account announces; it does not know what an SMS is\n"
 "interface AccountListener { void onPosted(Account account, Txn txn); }\n\n"
 "// State: the life is a fixed set of moves, checked before every write\n"
 "enum AccountStatus { ACTIVE, DORMANT, FROZEN, CLOSED }\n\n"
 "// the line with no pattern name, and the one that matters most\n"
 "Account first = fromId.compareTo(toId) < 0 ? from : to;   // a total order over the ids\n"),
]

